{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "productName": "Spiral",
  "branchName": "main",
  "overview": "SPIRAL is a self-iterating autonomous development loop (Research â†’ Implement â†’ Validate). This PRD drives Spiral to improve itself: integrating newly-written lib modules into the main loop, enhancing the setup wizard to educate users about every configurable option, and adding Chrome DevTools-powered visual validation.",
  "goals": [
    "Wire the four new lib modules (preflight, DAG, state machine, story review) into the live spiral.sh loop",
    "Enhance /spiral-init wizard to ask about ALL configurable options, educating users about what exists",
    "Achieve dynamic token-saving without sacrificing output quality via smarter model routing",
    "Enable Chrome DevTools MCP as a first-class visual validation tool in Phase V"
  ],
  "userStories": [
    {
      "id": "US-199",
      "title": "Export dependency graph as Graphviz DOT file for advanced rendering",
      "priority": "low",
      "description": "Mermaid covers basic graph needs but large PRDs with 100+ stories render poorly in it. Add a --format dot flag to spiral graph that outputs a Graphviz DOT file with cluster subgraphs grouping stories by priority tier. DOT files can be rendered by graphviz dot CLI or imported into draw.io for publication-quality diagrams.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
        "spiral graph --format dot produces a valid DOT digraph consumable by graphviz dot -Tsvg",
        "Nodes are coloured by status: pending=white, in_progress=yellow, passed=green, failed=red",
        "Priority clusters group nodes: subgraph cluster_critical, cluster_high, cluster_medium, cluster_low",
        "The output DOT file passes dot -Tsvg without warnings",
        "spiral graph --format dot | dot -Tsvg > graph.svg produces a readable SVG for a 50-story PRD"
      ],
      "technicalNotes": [
        "Graphviz DOT language reference: https://graphviz.org/doc/info/lang.html",
        "Use Python graphviz package for programmatic DOT generation",
        "Node color attribute uses HTML color names compatible with both dot and neato engines"
      ],
      "dependencies": [
        "US-195"
      ],
      "estimatedComplexity": "small",
      "passes": false
    },
    {
      "id": "US-200",
      "title": "Publish story-level OTel trace IDs to GitHub PR description",
      "priority": "low",
      "description": "SPIRAL creates GitHub PRs per story but the PR description currently contains no link to observability data. When OTel tracing is active and a trace ID is available for a story, append an Observability section to the PR body containing the trace ID and a direct link to the configured OTel UI (Jaeger, Zipkin, or Grafana Tempo). Closes the loop between code review and performance data.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
        "When SPIRAL_OTLP_UI_URL is set, generated PR descriptions include an Observability section with trace URL",
        "Trace URL is formatted as {SPIRAL_OTLP_UI_URL}/trace/{trace_id}",
        "If no trace ID is available the section is omitted and no placeholder is left",
        "PR description update is idempotent: re-running on an existing PR appends only if section is absent",
        "Integration test mocks the GitHub API and verifies the PR body contains the trace URL"
      ],
      "technicalNotes": [
        "OTel trace ID is available from the root span context; store it to story metadata after Phase R starts",
        "GitHub PR update uses gh pr edit --body or PATCH /repos/:owner/:repo/pulls/:pull_number",
        "Grafana Tempo trace URL format: {tempo_host}/explore with datasource=Tempo and query=traceId"
      ],
      "dependencies": [
        "US-184"
      ],
      "estimatedComplexity": "small",
      "passes": false
    },
    {
      "id": "US-214",
      "title": "Use git sparse-checkout in worker worktrees to reduce checkout footprint for large repos",
      "priority": "low",
      "description": "Configure git sparse-checkout in each worker's worktree so only the files listed in the story's filesTouch hint are checked out. In large monorepos, full worktree checkout can take significant time and disk space; sparse checkout reduces both by 60-90% for stories touching a small subset of files.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
        "When SPIRAL_SPARSE_CHECKOUT=true and filesTouch is non-empty, worker worktrees are created with git sparse-checkout set <files>",
        "If filesTouch is empty, sparse checkout is skipped and full checkout is used",
        "git sparse-checkout reapply is called when the story's filesTouch list is updated",
        "sparse-checkout mode is cone mode for performance",
        "Feature is disabled by default (opt-in via SPIRAL_SPARSE_CHECKOUT=true)"
      ],
      "technicalNotes": [
        "git sparse-checkout documentation: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-sparse-checkout",
        "Cone mode is significantly faster than non-cone: use git sparse-checkout init --cone",
        "Ensure sparse-checkout does not interfere with git worktree prune operations"
      ],
      "dependencies": [],
      "estimatedComplexity": "medium",
      "passes": false
    },
    {
      "id": "US-215",
      "title": "Add _humanNotes and _reviewedBy annotation fields to prd.json story schema",
      "priority": "low",
      "description": "Add two optional human-facing annotation fields to the prd.json story schema: _humanNotes (free-text string array for reviewer comments) and _reviewedBy (username/timestamp pairs). These fields are ignored by all automated pipeline stages but preserved through merges, giving humans a structured place to annotate stories without interfering with automation.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
        "_humanNotes: string[] and _reviewedBy: {by: string, at: string}[] are valid fields in prd.json stories and pass JSON Schema validation",
        "merge_stories.py preserves these fields when merging new research output (no clobber)",
        "spiral export-report includes _humanNotes in the story entry if non-empty",
        "The spiral_dashboard.py story detail view displays _humanNotes and _reviewedBy if present",
        "These fields are stripped from the story context sent to LLM to avoid prompt contamination"
      ],
      "technicalNotes": [
        "Add to prd.json JSON Schema file (created by the prd-schema story)",
        "In Pydantic model, mark fields as Optional[list] = None to keep backward compatibility",
        "Document in SPIRAL docs that underscore prefix conventionally marks non-automated fields"
      ],
      "dependencies": [],
      "estimatedComplexity": "small",
      "passes": false
    },
    {
      "id": "US-217",
      "title": "Add SPIRAL_COMMIT_SIGNING env var to enable GPG/SSH signing on AI-generated commits",
      "priority": "low",
      "description": "Add a SPIRAL_COMMIT_SIGNING env var that, when set to gpg or ssh, configures git to sign AI-generated commits using the user's configured GPG key or SSH signing key. Signed commits provide cryptographic proof that commits attributed to the AI agent actually originated from an authorized SPIRAL run, which is important for compliance in regulated environments.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
        "When SPIRAL_COMMIT_SIGNING=gpg, ralph.sh passes -c commit.gpgSign=true and -c gpg.program=<gpg> to git commit",
        "When SPIRAL_COMMIT_SIGNING=ssh, ralph.sh sets commit.gpgSign=true and gpg.format=ssh",
        "When SPIRAL_COMMIT_SIGNING is unset or empty, signing is not configured (default behavior unchanged)",
        "spiral-doctor verifies that the required signing key (gpg --list-secret-keys or SSH key) is available when SPIRAL_COMMIT_SIGNING is set",
        "A failed signing attempt aborts the commit with a clear error rather than committing unsigned"
      ],
      "technicalNotes": [
        "Git commit signing docs: https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Tools-Signing-Your-Work",
        "SSH signing requires git >= 2.34: https://calebhearth.com/sign-git-with-ssh",
        "Pass signing config via -c flags rather than modifying .git/config to keep it ephemeral"
      ],
      "dependencies": [],
      "estimatedComplexity": "small",
      "passes": false
    },
    {
      "id": "US-233",
      "title": "Add multi-agent failure attribution logging to identify root cause of cascade failures",
      "priority": "medium",
      "description": "When multiple Spiral workers run concurrently and one fails, it can cause downstream story failures through shared state (prd.json writes, git ref conflicts, port contention). Currently there is no structured attribution linking a cascade failure back to its root cause worker. Adding structured failure attribution logs — recording which worker ID triggered a cascade, what resource it was contending for, and which downstream stories were affected — makes incident diagnosis tractable.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
        "Each worker failure event includes: worker_id, story_id, failure_type (local|cascade), root_cause_worker_id (if cascade), contended_resource",
        "Cascade detection: if worker B fails within 30s of worker A's failure and shares a resource, B's failure is tagged as potential cascade",
        "'spiral diagnose --run <run-id>' prints a causal failure chain for the run",
        "Failure attribution data is stored in a structured failures section of the run's checkpoint JSON",
        "A test scenario with intentional worker conflict verifies cascade attribution is recorded"
      ],
      "technicalNotes": [
        "ICML 2025 multi-agent failure attribution research: https://syncedreview.com/2025/08/14/which-agent-causes-task-failures-and-whenresearchers-from-psu-and-duke-explores-automated-failure-attribution-of-llm-multi-agent-systems/",
        "Track shared resources per worker: worktree path, branch name, prd.json write lock, port allocations",
        "Use monotonic timestamps to establish happens-before relationships between worker events"
      ],
      "dependencies": [],
      "estimatedComplexity": "medium",
      "passes": false
    },
    {
      "id": "US-234",
      "title": "Add GitHub Actions job summary with per-story results table on each CI run",
      "priority": "low",
      "description": "CI runs currently produce pass/fail status with no structured summary visible in the GitHub UI. Writing a Markdown summary to $GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY with a table of story IDs, statuses, token costs, and durations gives reviewers an at-a-glance view of each iteration's output directly on the Actions run page, without needing to parse raw log lines.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
        "The CI workflow appends a Markdown table to $GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY after each spiral run",
        "Table columns: Story ID, Title, Status (pass/fail/skip), Duration (s), Token Cost ($)",
        "A summary footer shows total stories run, total cost, and total duration",
        "The summary step runs even if earlier steps fail (using 'if: always()')",
        "Summary size stays under 1 MiB (enforced by truncating to top 200 stories if needed)"
      ],
      "technicalNotes": [
        "GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY docs: https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/workflow-commands",
        "GitHub Flavored Markdown is supported; tables, bold, and checkboxes all render",
        "Generate summary from prd.json using jq or Python after each spiral iteration"
      ],
      "dependencies": [],
      "estimatedComplexity": "small",
      "passes": false
    },
    {
      "id": "US-235",
      "title": "Use jq --stream for prd.json parsing to support files exceeding 10 MB",
      "priority": "low",
      "description": "As Spiral accumulates hundreds of stories with embedded LLM outputs, prd.json can grow past 10 MB. jq's default mode reads the entire file into memory; on constrained CI runners this causes OOM kills. Migrating prd.json queries in shell scripts to jq --stream mode (combined with fromstream/truncate_stream) enables memory-efficient extraction without loading the full document.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
        "All jq invocations in spiral.sh and ralph.sh that read prd.json are audited; those reading top-level arrays are migrated to --stream mode",
        "A test script generates a synthetic 15 MB prd.json and verifies jq --stream extraction completes under 512 MB RSS",
        "Streaming queries use compact output (-c) to reduce intermediate allocation",
        "A comment in the script explains why --stream is used, for maintainability"
      ],
      "technicalNotes": [
        "jq streaming tutorial: https://devblog.songkick.com/parsing-ginormous-json-files-via-streaming-be6561ea8671",
        "Pattern: jq -cn --stream 'fromstream(1|truncate_stream(.,1))' prd.json | jq 'select(.id==\"US-123\")'",
        "jq 1.8.1 is the recommended version in 2026"
      ],
      "dependencies": [],
      "estimatedComplexity": "small",
      "passes": false
    },
    {
      "id": "US-236",
      "title": "Add bats-assert and bats-support libraries to existing bats test suite",
      "priority": "low",
      "description": "The existing bats tests use raw bash assertions ([ ... ] with manual error messages), which produce cryptic output on failure. The bats-assert and bats-support helper libraries provide readable assertion functions (assert_output, assert_line, assert_success, refute_output) that emit diff-style failure messages. Adopting them improves test maintainability and makes CI failure output actionable without running locally.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
        "bats-assert and bats-support are added as git submodules or npm devDependencies in the test directory",
        "All existing bats tests are migrated to use assert_output / assert_success / assert_failure",
        "At least one new test uses assert_line to check specific lines in multi-line output",
        "CI installs bats helper libraries as part of the test setup step",
        "Test failure output in CI shows the expected vs actual diff without additional tooling"
      ],
      "technicalNotes": [
        "bats-core repo: https://github.com/bats-core/bats-core",
        "bats-assert/bats-support are sub-projects under the bats-core GitHub org",
        "Install via: git submodule add https://github.com/bats-core/bats-assert test/lib/bats-assert"
      ],
      "dependencies": [],
      "estimatedComplexity": "small",
      "passes": false
    },
    {
      "id": "US-237",
      "title": "Add per-job GitHub Actions cache for uv/pip deps and pre-commit hooks",
      "priority": "low",
      "description": "CI jobs that reinstall Python dependencies and pre-commit environments from scratch on every run waste 2-5 minutes per run. Adding actions/cache steps keyed on the uv.lock (or requirements.txt) hash for Python deps and on the pre-commit config hash for hook environments eliminates these redundant installs, directly reducing CI cost and wait time.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
        "A cache step with key '${{ hashFiles(\"**/uv.lock\") }}' wraps Python dependency install in all CI jobs",
        "Pre-commit hook environments are cached with key '${{ hashFiles(\".pre-commit-config.yaml\") }}'",
        "Cache restore-keys provide fallback to nearest ancestor cache on miss",
        "CI run duration is measured before and after; at least 2 minutes saved on warm runs",
        "Cache invalidation is documented: changing uv.lock or pre-commit config busts the respective cache"
      ],
      "technicalNotes": [
        "actions/cache v4 is required (v2/v3 deprecated Feb 2025): https://github.com/actions/cache",
        "uv has its own cache dir (~/.cache/uv); key on uv.lock for lockfile-based installs",
        "Pre-commit cache dir: ~/.cache/pre-commit; separate cache key from Python deps"
      ],
      "dependencies": [],
      "estimatedComplexity": "small",
      "passes": false
    },
    {
      "id": "US-238",
      "title": "Structure bats tests to source functions under test for true unit coverage",
      "priority": "low",
      "description": "Current bats tests execute shell scripts as black boxes, meaning individual functions cannot be tested in isolation. Refactoring scripts to guard direct execution with 'if [[ \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\" == \"${0}\" ]]' allows bats tests to source the script and call individual functions, enabling true unit testing of helpers like worktree_prune, cost_check, and lock_cleanup with fine-grained assertions.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
        "All major shell scripts (spiral.sh, ralph.sh, and key utility scripts) are refactored with BASH_SOURCE guard around their main execution block",
        "At least 5 internal helper functions have dedicated bats unit tests that source the script and call the function directly",
        "Existing integration-style bats tests continue to pass unchanged",
        "A new 'unit' test suite target runs only the function-level tests (fast, no side effects)",
        "CI runs both unit and integration bats suites and reports results separately"
      ],
      "technicalNotes": [
        "BASH_SOURCE guard pattern: https://www.code-sage.com/blog/devops/2024/10/26/Automated-Testing-for-BASH-with-BATS.html",
        "Move main logic to run_main() function, guard with: if [[ \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\" == \"${0}\" ]]; then run_main \"$@\"; fi",
        "Use bats-mock (https://github.com/grayhemp/bats-mock) to stub external commands like git, curl, jq"
      ],
      "dependencies": [],
      "estimatedComplexity": "medium",
      "passes": false
    },
    {
      "id": "US-248",
      "title": "Implement LLM-as-Judge continuous quality evaluation for phase outputs",
      "priority": "medium",
      "description": "Beyond pass/fail metrics, phase outputs should be evaluated for semantic quality. After each Phase R and Phase I completion, a cheap LLM-as-Judge call scores the output for completeness, accuracy, and relevance, storing scores in the checkpoint for trend analysis and early detection of model degradation.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
        "After Phase R completes, ralph.sh invokes an LLM-as-Judge prompt scoring the research output on a 1-5 scale for relevance and completeness",
        "After Phase I completes, the judge scores implementation quality: follows acceptance criteria, no obvious errors",
        "Scores are stored in the checkpoint under _qualityScores.{phase}: {score, rationale, timestamp}",
        "The dashboard displays average quality scores per phase across the current iteration",
        "Low scores (below SPIRAL_QUALITY_THRESHOLD) emit a warning but do not block progression"
      ],
      "technicalNotes": [
        "LLM-as-Judge: 'minimal overhead ($0.06, 14.7s)' vs Agent-as-Judge '$0.96, 913s'; use LLM-Judge for continuous monitoring (arxiv 2512.12791)",
        "Use a fast/cheap model (haiku or flash) for judging to minimize cost",
        "Quality scores feed into the production failure telemetry feedback loop"
      ],
      "dependencies": [],
      "estimatedComplexity": "medium",
      "passes": false
    },
    {
      "id": "US-249",
      "title": "Add tool parameter semantic validation before LLM-directed tool calls",
      "priority": "medium",
      "description": "Agent tool failures are the most common failure mode in complex scenarios, often caused by incorrect parameter mapping. Before executing tool calls in ralph.sh, a validation layer checks parameter types and semantics against a tool schema registry, surfacing parameter errors before they cause hard-to-debug downstream failures.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
        "A .spiral/tool-schema.json file defines expected parameter types for the top 10 tools invoked by workers (git, python, bats, jq, curl)",
        "ralph.sh validates extracted tool call parameters against the schema before execution",
        "Invalid parameters produce a structured error logged to checkpoint under _toolErrors: {tool, parameter, expected, received, timestamp}",
        "A bats test verifies that a malformed tool call is caught at the validation layer and not executed",
        "Schema validation adds no more than 50ms overhead per tool call"
      ],
      "technicalNotes": [
        "Tools pillar finding: 'incorrect parameter mapping (region name vs. instance ID)' is the most common tool failure mode (arxiv 2512.12791)",
        "Keep validation in bash/python (deterministic) not LLM to avoid recursive validation cost",
        "Start with top 5 tools: git subcommand validation, python module paths, bats test file existence checks"
      ],
      "dependencies": [],
      "estimatedComplexity": "medium",
      "passes": false
    },
    {
      "id": "US-251",
      "title": "Extend spiral.sh --replay with --from-phase and --hint for targeted recovery",
      "priority": "medium",
      "description": "The existing --replay flag re-runs a story from scratch. Adding --from-phase and --hint parameters enables targeted recovery: re-execute only from a specific phase using additional operator-supplied context, reusing prior checkpoint state to skip already-completed phases and avoid redundant LLM API calls.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
        "spiral.sh --replay STORY_ID --from-phase PHASE_LETTER re-executes a story starting from the specified phase",
        "The replay reuses the existing worktree and checkpoint state, skipping phases already marked complete before PHASE_LETTER",
        "An optional --hint 'text' flag injects extra context into the Phase I system prompt for the replay run",
        "Replay attempts are tracked in the checkpoint under _replayHistory: [{timestamp, fromPhase, hint}]",
        "A bats test verifies replay starts from the correct phase and does not re-execute completed phases"
      ],
      "technicalNotes": [
        "From Vellum guide: 'track and replay tasks with new instructions to understand and improve agent behavior'",
        "Phase letter validation: reject invalid letters and print usage before attempting replay",
        "Hint injection: prepend hint as a system prompt addendum, not user turn, to avoid prompt injection risk"
      ],
      "dependencies": [],
      "estimatedComplexity": "medium",
