    {
      "id": "US-257",
      "title": "Tune parallel worker concurrency by operation type (CPU vs network vs disk)",
      "priority": "low",
      "description": "SPIRAL launches all workers with the same parallelism setting regardless of operation type, causing I/O thrashing on disk-intensive phases. Tuning concurrency by operation type prevents saturation and improves overall throughput by matching parallelism to the bottleneck resource.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
        "Three separate env vars replace a single SPIRAL_WORKER_COUNT: SPIRAL_CPU_WORKERS (default: nproc), SPIRAL_NET_WORKERS (default: 4), SPIRAL_DISK_WORKERS (default: 2)",
        "spiral.sh selects the appropriate limit by phase: Phase R (network, SPIRAL_NET_WORKERS), Phase I/V (CPU, SPIRAL_CPU_WORKERS), Phase M (disk, SPIRAL_DISK_WORKERS)",
        "The active concurrency limit is logged at phase start: 'Launching N workers (network phase cap)'",
        "A bats test verifies no more than SPIRAL_NET_WORKERS workers run concurrently during Phase R",
        "Backward compatibility: setting SPIRAL_WORKER_COUNT still applies as a cap across all three limits"
      ],
      "technicalNotes": [
        "gitcheatsheet.dev: 'CPU-bound: match parallelism to core count; Network: limit to 2-4 workers regardless of cores; Disk-bound: restrict to 1-2 workers to prevent I/O thrashing'",
        "Benchmark: four parallel workers reduced test execution from 12 minutes to 5 minutes (58% reduction)",
        "Phase classification: R=network, I=CPU+disk, V=CPU, M=disk"
      ],
      "dependencies": [],
      "estimatedComplexity": "medium",
      "passes": false
    },
    {
      "id": "US-258",
      "title": "Add dashboard story changelog drilldown view from prd.json _changelog",
      "priority": "low",
      "description": "Reconstruct the full lifecycle of any story in the dashboard from _changelog entries, showing every status change, field edit, and retry with timestamps and the responsible script. This enables operators to audit why a story ended up in its current state without digging through raw JSON.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
        "The dashboard's per-story drilldown page includes a 'Story Changelog' tab sourced from prd.json._changelog filtered to that storyId",
        "Each changelog entry is rendered as a timeline row: timestamp, changed field, old→new value, author script",
        "The timeline is sorted chronologically and supports filtering by field name",
        "A 'copy as markdown' button exports the story changelog as a markdown table",
        "The changelog tab loads within 500ms even for stories with 100+ changelog entries"
      ],
      "technicalNotes": [
        "Depends on the 'Add living PRD changelog tracking story field mutations' story for the _changelog field",
        "Render using the existing HTML details element pattern already in the dashboard codebase",
        "Filter implementation: client-side JavaScript, no additional server endpoints required"
      ],
      "dependencies": [
        "US-256"
      ],
      "estimatedComplexity": "small",
      "passes": false
    },
    {
      "id": "US-267",
      "title": "Instrument worker phase transitions with OTel gen_ai.agent spans",
      "priority": "medium",
      "description": "The existing OTel integration emits token-level gen_ai.usage.* counters but does not model each worker as a traceable agent entity with phase-level spans. Adopting the OpenTelemetry GenAI agent span conventions (gen_ai.agent.name, gen_ai.agent.id, invoke_agent span names) lets distributed tracing tools reconstruct the full R-I-V-T-M-G lifecycle per story as a trace tree, enabling root-cause analysis of slow or failing phases.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
        "Each worker emits a root span named 'invoke_agent {worker-name}' with gen_ai.agent.id set to the worker ID and gen_ai.agent.name set to 'spiral-worker'",
        "Each phase (R, I, V, T, M, G) is a child span named '{phase}-phase {story-id}' with gen_ai.operation.name and gen_ai.request.model attributes",
        "gen_ai.conversation.id is set to the story ID to correlate all phases of a story across the trace",
        "Spans are exported via OTLP to the endpoint configured in OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT",
        "The existing otel_run_context.json is updated to include the trace ID and agent span ID for cross-referencing",
        "A bats test verifies that span JSON output contains all required gen_ai.agent.* attributes"
      ],
      "technicalNotes": [
        "OTel GenAI agent span spec: https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/gen-ai/gen-ai-agent-spans/",
        "Required attributes: gen_ai.operation.name, gen_ai.provider.name; conditionally required: gen_ai.agent.name, gen_ai.agent.id",
        "gen_ai.conversation.id maps naturally to prd.json story ID for full-trace correlation",
        "Use opentelemetry-sdk Python package in spiral_dashboard.py or a separate otel_emitter.py helper"
      ],
      "dependencies": [],
      "estimatedComplexity": "medium",
      "passes": false
    },
    {
      "id": "US-268",
      "title": "Add gen_ai.client.operation.duration histogram for per-phase LLM latency",
      "priority": "medium",
      "description": "The OTel GenAI metrics specification defines gen_ai.client.operation.duration (histogram, seconds) and gen_ai.server.time_to_first_token as standard latency metrics, but SPIRAL currently only emits token count counters. Adding latency histograms per phase and model gives operators percentile-level visibility (p50/p95/p99) into which phases are slowest, enabling targeted prompt or model optimisation.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
        "gen_ai.client.operation.duration histogram is emitted for each LLM call with gen_ai.operation.name, gen_ai.request.model, and gen_ai.provider.name attributes",
        "gen_ai.server.time_to_first_token is recorded for streaming responses where the first chunk timestamp is measurable",
        "Metrics are exported alongside existing token counters via the same OTLP endpoint",
        "spiral_dashboard.py displays p50 and p95 phase latency in the story detail panel",
        "A unit test asserts that the histogram is updated on each simulated LLM call with the correct attribute set"
      ],
      "technicalNotes": [
        "OTel GenAI metrics spec: https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/gen-ai/gen-ai-metrics/",
        "gen_ai.client.operation.duration is Histogram with unit 's' and status Required",
        "gen_ai.server.time_to_first_token is Histogram with unit 's' and status Recommended",
        "Use opentelemetry-sdk MeterProvider; record start/end timestamps around each API call"
      ],
      "dependencies": [],
      "estimatedComplexity": "small",
      "passes": false
    },
    {
      "id": "US-269",
      "title": "Add per-worktree git config isolation to stamp AI commits with worker identity",
      "priority": "medium",
      "description": "Worker worktrees inherit the global git user.name and user.email, meaning AI-generated commits appear attributed to the human developer's identity. Setting a per-worktree git config that stamps commits as a distinct SPIRAL worker identity provides clear audit trails distinguishing AI commits from human commits and prevents accidental exposure of developer PII in public repositories.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
        "When spiral.sh creates a worktree, it immediately runs 'git -C <worktree-path> config user.name' and 'git -C <worktree-path> config user.email' scoped to that worktree only",
        "Default values are configurable via SPIRAL_WORKER_GIT_NAME (default: 'SPIRAL Worker') and SPIRAL_WORKER_GIT_EMAIL (default: 'spiral-worker@localhost')",
        "The trailer 'AI-Generated-By: SPIRAL/<worker-id>' is appended to all AI-authored commit messages via git interpret-trailers",
        "A bats test checks that commits made from a worker worktree carry the worker identity, not the global git config values",
        "The spiral status output shows the git author identity used per active worker"
      ],
      "technicalNotes": [
        "Use 'git -C <path> config user.name <value>' to set config local to the worktree (not --global)",
        "Git worktrees share .git objects but each has its own config layer via the worktree-specific gitdir",
        "AI-Generated-By trailer follows Git trailer convention (RFC-compliant key: value format)",
        "Reference: https://medium.com/@richardhightower/git-worktree-isolation-in-claude-code-parallel-development-without-the-chaos-262e12b85cc5"
      ],
      "dependencies": [],
      "estimatedComplexity": "small",
      "passes": false
    },
    {
      "id": "US-270",
      "title": "Compute and store story quality score at ingestion based on acceptance criteria richness",
      "priority": "medium",
      "description": "Stories enter the queue with wildly varying acceptance criteria quality — some have zero testable criteria, others contain only vague phrases like 'works correctly'. A quality scorer runs at ingestion time and assigns a 0-100 score based on measurable signals: number of acceptance criteria, presence of specific numeric thresholds, absence of vague language, and completeness of technicalNotes. Low-quality stories are flagged before any worker is assigned.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
        "A score_story_quality(story) function returns a QualityScore with: total (0-100), breakdown { criteria_count_pts, specificity_pts, measurability_pts, technical_notes_pts, source_pts }, and issues list",
        "Scoring: +20 for >= 3 acceptance criteria; +5 per criterion containing a number/threshold/percentage (up to +20); -10 per criterion containing a vague term ('works', 'correctly', 'properly', 'good', 'better', 'appropriate'); +20 for non-empty technicalNotes; +20 for a source URL",
        "Stories with quality score < 40 are assigned status 'needs-refinement' and are not dispatched to workers",
        "spiral status shows a quality score column (numeric, colour-coded) per story",
        "The _qualityScore field is written to prd.json as an annotation field not editable by humans",
        "A unit test asserts a story with no acceptance criteria and only vague language scores below 40"
      ],
      "technicalNotes": [
        "Vague term list: ['works', 'correctly', 'properly', 'good', 'better', 'nice', 'easy', 'simple', 'appropriate', 'reasonable']",
        "Use regex r'\\b\\d+(\\.\\d+)?\\s*(ms|MB|GB|%|s|seconds|tokens|requests)\\b' to detect measurable thresholds",
        "_qualityScore is stored alongside existing annotation fields like _humanNotes and _changelog",
        "Reference quality benchmarking from JSONSchemaBench: https://arxiv.org/html/2501.10868v3"
      ],
      "dependencies": [],
      "estimatedComplexity": "small",
      "passes": false
    },
    {
      "id": "US-271",
      "title": "Add Textual-based interactive TUI mode to spiral_dashboard.py",
      "priority": "medium",
      "description": "The current spiral_dashboard.py renders a static Rich table requiring manual refresh and offering no interactivity. Replacing the polling loop with a Textual application enables keyboard navigation between stories, inline live log streaming for the selected worker, and real-time auto-updating panels — eliminating the need for multiple terminal tabs during monitoring sessions.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
        "Running 'python spiral_dashboard.py --tui' launches a Textual app with a stories table panel and a worker log panel",
        "Arrow keys navigate the story list; pressing Enter on a story opens a detail panel showing acceptanceCriteria, current phase, driftScore, qualityScore, and SAST status",
        "The log panel live-tails .spiral/workers/<selected-worker-id>/ralph-run.log using async file watching, updating without user action",
        "Press 'q' to quit, 'r' to force-refresh all data, 'p' to pause/resume auto-refresh (default interval: 2s)",
        "The TUI degrades gracefully to the existing Rich table mode when TERM is 'dumb' or stdout is not a TTY",
        "A non-interactive unit test instantiates the Textual App and verifies the stories table widget is populated correctly from a fixture prd.json"
      ],
      "technicalNotes": [
        "Textual achieves 120 FPS renders via Rich segment trees with delta-updates of dirty regions only",
        "Use Textual for the interactive app framework; embed Rich renderables inside Textual widgets for static sections",
        "Async log tail: asyncio + aiofiles for non-blocking reads; update a Textual Log widget on new lines",
        "Reference: https://johal.in/textual-tui-widgets-python-rich-terminal-user-interfaces-apps-2025/"
      ],
      "dependencies": [],
      "estimatedComplexity": "large",
      "passes": false
    },
    {
      "id": "US-272",
      "title": "Implement contract-based integration tests for each SPIRAL phase boundary",
      "priority": "medium",
      "description": "Phase handoffs in SPIRAL (Phase R output to Phase I input, Phase V output to Gate decision) are untested end-to-end; a bug in the Phase R output format silently breaks Phase I without a clear error. Contract-style integration tests with bats exercise each of the six phase boundaries using fixture inputs and assert the output contract (schema and key fields), catching regressions before they reach production workers.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
        "A test/integration/ directory contains bats tests for each of the 6 phase transitions: R->I, I->V, V->T, T->M, M->G, G->done",
        "Each test sources a fixture JSON file (test/fixtures/phase_<N>_output.json) as input to the next phase and asserts the output satisfies the phase's documented contract",
        "Contract assertions include: correct exit code, expected output file(s) created, required JSON keys present, status field updated correctly in prd.json",
        "Tests use bats-assert for readable assertion failures (assert_output, assert_file_exists)",
        "CI runs the integration tests in a dedicated matrix job after unit tests pass",
        "A test specifically verifies that a malformed Phase R output missing the researchSummary key causes Phase I to exit with code 1 and a descriptive error message"
      ],
      "technicalNotes": [
        "Contract testing concept: https://pactflow.io/blog/ai-automation-part-1/",
        "bats-assert + bats-support: https://github.com/bats-core/bats-assert",
        "Fixture files avoid LLM calls in CI by mocking API endpoints with environment variable stubs",
        "Phase contracts should be codified as JSON Schema in test/contracts/phase_<N>_contract.json"
      ],
      "dependencies": [],
      "estimatedComplexity": "large",
      "passes": false
    },
    {
      "id": "US-273",
      "title": "Add structured error taxonomy with exit code ranges and inline remediation hints",
      "priority": "medium",
      "description": "SPIRAL currently exits with generic error messages and undifferentiated exit codes, making it difficult in CI or production to determine whether a failure is a config issue, an API error, a Git error, or a worker crash. A structured error taxonomy assigns each known failure mode a unique code, an exit code range by category, and a human-readable remediation hint with the exact fix command — turning opaque failures into self-diagnosing outputs.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
        "An ERROR_CATALOG (Python dict or bash associative array) maps error codes to: category, message template, exit_code, remediation_hint, and docs_url",
        "Error categories and exit code ranges: Config (E1xx, exit 1x), API (E2xx, exit 2x), Git (E3xx, exit 3x), Worker (E4xx, exit 4x), Schema (E5xx, exit 5x)",
        "Every exit point in spiral.sh and Python scripts calls spiral_exit(code, **context) which prints: '[SPIRAL-E-<code>] <message>\\nFix: <remediation_hint>\\nDocs: <docs_url>'",
        "Errors are written to spiral_events.jsonl as structured JSON with error_code, category, context, and timestamp fields",
        "A bats test triggers each of the 5 most common error codes and asserts the correct exit code range and message format",
        "The spiral status command shows the last error code per worker in a 'Last Error' column"
      ],
      "technicalNotes": [
        "Error message design principles: context (where) + specific details (what) + actionable guidance (how to fix) — https://florian-kraemer.net/software-architecture/2025/05/06/Designing-Effective-Error-Messages.html",
        "Exit code ranges extend sysexits.h conventions (64-78 system) with SPIRAL-specific ranges (80-99)",
        "Remediation hints must include the exact command to run, not just a prose description",
        "Cross-reference error codes in GitHub Actions job summaries for CI visibility"
      ],
      "dependencies": [],
      "estimatedComplexity": "medium",
      "passes": false
    },
    {
      "id": "US-274",
      "title": "Add role-based story assignment and approval workflow for multi-user teams",
      "priority": "low",
      "description": "SPIRAL is currently single-operator; all stories are owned by the same author and all workers run under the same credentials. A lightweight role system (owner, reviewer, operator) in prd.json stories enables team use: owners author stories, reviewers approve quality scores before dispatch, and operators monitor workers — providing the audit trail and access boundaries that enterprise team adoption requires.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
        "prd.json stories gain three new optional fields: _assignedTo (string, GitHub username), _reviewedBy (string, GitHub username), _approvalStatus (enum: pending|approved|rejected)",
        "spiral assign <story-id> <username> sets _assignedTo and records the action in _changelog with timestamp and actor",
        "spiral approve <story-id> sets _reviewedBy to SPIRAL_USER env var and changes _approvalStatus to 'approved', enabling dispatch",
        "Stories with quality score < 40 are automatically set to _approvalStatus 'pending' and require explicit 'spiral approve' before worker dispatch",
        "A webhook event is emitted to SPIRAL_NOTIFY_WEBHOOK when _approvalStatus changes, including story ID, reviewer, verdict, and timestamp",
        "The spiral status table shows _assignedTo and _approvalStatus columns when SPIRAL_SHOW_TEAM_COLUMNS=true"
      ],
      "technicalNotes": [
        "Multiplayer AI pattern: permission-aware behavior respecting team boundaries — https://thenewstack.io/the-next-era-of-ai-from-single-user-to-team-collaboration/",
        "SPIRAL_USER env var defaults to output of 'git config user.name'",
        "Role enforcement is advisory (logged, not cryptographically enforced) in v1; pairs with HMAC webhook signing for tamper-evidence",
        "Integrates with existing _humanNotes and _changelog annotation fields already in prd.json schema"
      ],
      "dependencies": [],
      "estimatedComplexity": "medium",
      "passes": false
    },
    {
      "id": "US-275",
      "title": "Generate Markdown documentation stubs and CHANGELOG entries on Gate pass",
      "priority": "low",
      "description": "Every story that passes the Gate represents a completed, tested feature whose documentation (README sections, changelog entries) is never automatically created. Generating documentation stubs from the story's title, description, acceptanceCriteria, and technicalNotes at Gate-pass time creates a reviewable first-draft, reducing documentation debt without requiring an additional LLM call.",
      "acceptanceCriteria": [
